work_order.approach:
Three edits, two files. The test helper gains an offset comparison; `mainOf` regains its `id` requirement; the stylesheet gains one `@media (forced-colors: active)` block appended after the `#main:focus` rule, and its comment is rewritten so the omission-or-restoration reads as a decision.

**1. Ordering — test/web/pages.test.js:146-159.** Capture the `<main>` MATCH (not just its attribute string, so `.index` is available) and the skip link's match index, then require the link to come first. `matchAll` results carry `.index`, so the two offsets are directly comparable. Keep the existing rationale intact: this is not a pin on the link being the *first* element, only on it preceding `<main>`, so the brand and the nav may still sort before it (that is what the reviewer's fix says, and the third pin case below proves it). Order the `&&` chain so `main.index` is only read after `mainAttrs` is known non-empty — I confirmed the chain short-circuits to `false` rather than throwing on a document with no `<main>`, no `<a>`, or an empty string.

**2. mainOf — test/web/pages.test.js:116.** Use the reviewer's regex verbatim. It is attribute-order independent (so the `tabindex` #52 added still matches) and keeps the heading-hierarchy assertion tied to the skip link's actual target.

**3. Forced colors — src/web/styles.css:116-118.** Append immediately after the existing rule:

```css
@media (forced-colors: active) {
  #main:focus {
    outline: 2px solid CanvasText;
  }
}
```

`CanvasText` is a CSS system colour, so it resolves to the user's chosen foreground rather than a hardcoded value — the same reason the rest of the file uses tokens. The block must come AFTER the bare rule to win on source order at equal specificity (`#main:focus` is 1,1,0 in both). I verified against the patched stylesheet that the existing test at test/web/pages.test.js:204-209 still matches the first `#main:focus` rule and still sees `outline: none` — the carve-out does not disturb it, because that regex takes the first match and the plain rule still comes first.

Append to the rule's comment (after the existing 'nothing inside main is affected' sentence) a short paragraph recording *why*: the suppression is about the authored ring, and in forced-colors Chromium honours `outline: none` and substitutes no system colour, so the carve-out restores one there. The reviewer explicitly asked that the omission read as a decision rather than an oversight; this comment makes it one either way.

**What this deliberately does not do.** No change to `pages.js` — the shipped markup already places the skip link before `<main>` (pages.js:68 vs 77); only the lock was missing. No change to the `outline: none` itself: the ring-around-1100px-of-main problem the rule solves is real, and the carve-out is additive.
